# p_8f4c — Marco takes personal items only: keys, shoes, wallet, water bottle, notebook, backpack, jacket, sunglasses (fork of p_b7c2)

The parent document (p_b7c2) claimed Marco packs his lunchbox, vitamins, and journal before leaving for work. The evidence refutes this: on weekdays between 9:00 and 17:00, lunchbox_marco is found at cupboard_k1 on all 6 looks, vitamins_marco at counter_k1 on 5 of 6, and journal_marco at desk_b1 on 5 of 7. These items stay in the house while Marco is at work.

What actually goes out with Marco: his keys (last seen at 12:00, gone by 14:00), shoes (same), wallet (same), water_bottle (same), notebook (same), backpack (same), jacket (same), and sunglasses (same). All of these personal items are present in the morning (00:00–12:00) and absent from 14:00 onward on weekdays. The "weekday 9-17h" data shows 2 found / 5 not-found for each, consistent with them being present until about 13:00 and gone after.

What changed from the parent: the OUT_OF_HOUSE blocks for lunchbox_marco, vitamins_marco, and journal_marco are removed. New OUT_OF_HOUSE blocks are added for keys_marco, shoes_marco, wallet_marco, water_bottle_marco, notebook_marco, backpack_marco, jacket_marco, and sunglasses_marco. The claims are rewritten to test the items that actually travel. The rest of the household routine is unchanged.

What sets this hypothesis apart: between 14:00 and 22:00 on a weekday, keys_marco, shoes_marco, wallet_marco, water_bottle_marco, notebook_marco, backpack_marco, jacket_marco, and sunglasses_marco are OUT_OF_HOUSE, while lunchbox_marco is at cupboard_k1, vitamins_marco is at counter_k1, and journal_marco is at desk_b1. Finding any of the personal items in the house during that window refutes this document; finding the lunchbox, vitamins, or journal out of the house also refutes it.

